    {
      "id": "redis-9323",
      "scenario": "bugfix",
      "language": "C",
      "pr_url": "https://github.com/redis/redis/pull/9323",
      "title": "Replica keep serving data during repl-diskless-load=swapdb for better availability",
      "status": "completed_with_warnings",
      "duration_ms": 0,
      "references": [
        {
          "file": "src/server.c",
          "line": 5437,
          "body": "i think it may be a cleaner approach to turn on both `server.loading` and `server.async_loading` in our scenario. this way, all the existing code that checks the `loading` flag will not need a change, and we'll only need to add a few exceptions like `server.loading \u0026\u0026 !server.async_loading`.",
          "cid": 684999246,
          "author": "oranagra",
          "dimension": "design",
          "kind": "design"
        },
        {
          "file": "src/rdb.c",
          "line": 2502,
          "body": "Here's a list of scenarios: 1. a replica gets disconnected and gets a full-sync when it reconnects. 2. a replica was connected to one master and got redirected to a new master (of the same replication chain) and got a full sync for some reason. 3. redis was restarted from rdb file (after a graceful shutdown), and has replicaof config, attempted a partial sync with the replid it got from the rdb file, but gets a full sync. 4. the replica was a master till now and got some data from that era, then gets a REPLICAOF command and does a full-sync. 5. the replica started empty and is attempting its first sync ever. 6. the replica was replicating from a certain master, and is now full-syncing from another master that's completely unrelated to the previous one. i think the first 3 scenarios want to allow clients to read the data, but on the last 3 we don't! i think the rule here is that if the current dataset in the db represents a valid point in time of the master we're full-syncing from, then we wanna allow reads, but if it doesn't represent a certain point in time of that master, then we don't want to serve reads.",
          "cid": 685941509,
          "author": "oranagra",
          "dimension": "design",
          "kind": "design"
        },
        {
          "file": "src/db.c",
          "line": 497,
          "body": "This change is gonna break modules that use the `RedisModuleEvent_ReplBackup` mechanism anyway, so i suggest the following: 1. We deprecate `RedisModuleEvent_ReplBackup` (starting redis 7.0 we never fire that event). 2. We create a new API named `RedisModuleEvent_ReplAsync`, holding 3 sub-events; STARTED, COMPLETED, ABORTED. 3. We add another module flag for `RedisModule_SetModuleOptions` (REDISMODULE_OPTIONS_HANDLE_REPL_ASYNC). 4. in replication.c, if there are modules loaded which registered a data type and didn't declare they're supporting this, we fall back to the alternative replication method.",
          "cid": 691928309,
          "author": "oranagra",
          "dimension": "compatibility",
          "kind": "design"
        },
        {
          "file": "src/db.c",
          "line": 1328,
          "body": "this function is not as generic as it seems (by its name), the implementation and behavior are quite specific for placing the newly loaded temp db at the main active one, and the old active one in the temp. so i think we need a better name for it, and we certainly need a beefier doc comment.",
          "cid": 685446527,
          "author": "oranagra",
          "dimension": "maintainability",
          "kind": "suggestion"
        },
        {
          "file": "src/db.c",
          "line": 499,
          "body": "The slot-to-key array needs to be free'd somewhere before the tempDb is free'd. Flush just memsets it to zero. Maybe we can replace slotToKeyFlush with two functions: slotToKeyInit (allocates it) and slotToKeyDestroy (frees it)?",
          "cid": 725662202,
          "author": "zuiderkwast",
          "dimension": "correctness",
          "kind": "defect"
        },
        {
          "file": "src/db.c",
          "line": 1322,
          "body": "This code doesn't swap them. It just copies one to the other. Don't we want to swap them like we do for dict, expires, avg_ttl and expires_cursor below? Also, we don't need memcpy here. We can just swap the pointers.",
          "cid": 725663704,
          "author": "zuiderkwast",
          "dimension": "correctness",
          "kind": "defect"
        }
      ],
      "findings": [],
      "reference_verdicts": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "verdict": "not_covered",
          "reason": "No system findings were produced, so this design suggestion about unifying loading/async_loading flags is unaddressed."
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "verdict": "not_covered",
          "reason": "No system findings were produced, so this design concern about which replication scenarios should serve reads is unaddressed."
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "verdict": "not_covered",
          "reason": "No system findings were produced, so the module API/ReplBackup deprecation suggestion is unaddressed."
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "verdict": "not_covered",
          "reason": "No system findings were produced, so the naming/doc-comment suggestion is unaddressed."
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "verdict": "not_covered",
          "reason": "No system findings were produced, so the slot-to-key leak concern is unaddressed."
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "verdict": "not_covered",
          "reason": "No system findings were produced, so the swap-vs-copy defect is unaddressed."
        }
      ],
